<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Auto-bidding is central to computational advertising, achieving notable commercial success by optimizing advertisers’ bids within economic constraints.
Recently, large generative models show potential to revolutionize auto-bidding by generating bids that could flexibly adapt to complex, competitive environments. Among them, diffusers stand out for their ability to address sparse-reward challenges by focusing on trajectory-level accumulated rewards, as well as their explainable capability, <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, planning a future trajectory of states and executing bids accordingly.
However, diffusers struggle with generation uncertainty, particularly regarding dynamic legitimacy between adjacent states, which can lead to poor bids and further cause significant loss of ad impression opportunities when competing with other advertisers in a highly competitive auction environment.
To address it, we propose a <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ausal auto-<span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B</span>idding method based on a <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>iffusion completer-aligner framework, termed <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CBD</span>.
Firstly, we augment the diffusion training process with an extra random variable <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, where the model observes <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-length historical sequences with the goal of completing the remaining sequence, thereby enhancing the generated sequences’ dynamic legitimacy. Then, we employ a trajectory-level return model to refine the generated trajectories, aligning more closely with advertisers’ objectives.
<span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Experimental results across diverse settings demonstrate that our approach not only achieves superior performance on large-scale auto-bidding benchmarks, such as a 29.9% improvement in conversion value in the challenging sparse-reward auction setting, but also delivers significant improvements on the Kuaishou online advertising platform, including a 2.0% increase in target cost.</span></p>
  
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03348v1/nips_cbd_main_fig_cropped.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/253;" width="476" height="253" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S0.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of our CBD method: At each timestep <math id="S0.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, a completer first receives a “Query” (observed states <math id="S0.F1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{0:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0:t}</annotation></semantics></math> reflecting ad impression features and auction status) and outputs an “Answer” (generated future states <math id="S0.F1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{t+1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t+1:T}</annotation></semantics></math>) within the dynamic legitimate area; The aligner then refines the generation to achieve closer alignment with the expected trajectory property; Finally, a bid is determined based on the refined trajectory and sent to the auction to compete with other advertisers.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid digital transformation of commerce has significantly expanded the reach of online advertising platforms, making them essential for engaging audiences and driving sales <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.
With the continuous influx of ad impression opportunities on the platform, real-time auto-bidding for display advertising, which emerged in 2009, has become a dominant paradigm in computational advertising <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
Specifically, when an ad impression opportunity is generated from a user’s visits, a bid request for this opportunity, including its features like user, context, and auction status, is broadcast to numerous advertisers via hosting a real-time ad auction. Then, a specific auto-bidding algorithm is selected by the advertiser to determine a bid price in real-time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
The goal of auto-bidding is to maximize the total accumulated impression value for an advertiser while adhering to some economic constraints, such as cost-per-conversion (CPC), throughout an entire bidding period, typically a day <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
Previous auto-bidding strategies can be categorized into rule-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, prediction-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, and sequential decision-making methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> based on reinforcement learning (RL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.
Recently, generative models have achieved remarkable success in tasks such as NLP and CV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Given the immense commercial value, it is worthwhile to explore generative models’ potential to revolutionize auto-bidding by directly generating bids that can handle the complex advertising environment while adapting to the economic conditions of diverse advertisers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In existing literature, there are two major paradigms employing generative models for sequential decision-making:
decision transformer (DT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> and diffuser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>.
However, DT faces reliability as it directly outputs a bid without showcasing the reasoning process. Even when attempting long-term planning, it suffers from accumulated errors due to its auto-regressive generation.
Thus, DT complicates the diagnosis and rectification of errors in industrial auto-bidding services.
Moreover, DT relies on the single-step reward function, which can lead to poor performance in scenarios with sparse rewards <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, which is common in online advertising, such as in-app purchase (IAP).
Diffuser, instead, offers a promising alternative by generating an entire trajectory of states based on a specified expected property as a condition. Actions are then executed according to the trajectory, resembling a planning-based decision-making paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.
This approach significantly enhances explainability by revealing the model’s thought process, <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, the planned trajectory.
Additionally, the diffuser learns a joint distribution between an entire trajectory and its accumulated rewards, which helps alleviate the sparse reward issues, leading to a more general usage than DT in auto-bidding.
A pioneering work, DiffBid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, supports this by successfully deploying a decision diffuser for auto-bidding in a small private industry dataset, achieving superior performance than RL and DT.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, directly applying diffusers could lead to poor performance in large-scale auctions with a large number of advertisers, millions of impression opportunities, and complex, uncertain impression distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.
This could be due to diffusers’ inherent generation uncertainty issue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 Performance Comparison with Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Specifically, the planned next state may not be achievable from the current state, leading to a dynamic legitimacy issue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>.
Additionally, randomly sampling from prior distributions in the denoising process could result in multiple possible generations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, but not all align well with the advertiser’s objectives.
The situation worsens in large-scale auctions, where the tolerance for bids is minimal due to high competitiveness among advertisers and complex ad impression distributions. Suboptimal bids can lead to significant loss of impression opportunities or spend waste.
Previous works have tried to address this issue in offline RL tasks, typically involving single-agent, non-competitive tasks like locomotion control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>.
AdaptDiffuser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> and MetaDiffuser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> use a forward dynamic model (FDM) for enhancing legitimacy, but a poor base model could make FDM useless <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.
DiffuserLite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> revises training process as generative interpolation by training multiple diffusers, but it could limit overall planning.
Adopting causal network architectures for training a diffuser, such as DiT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> with causal attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, is closely related to addressing this issue, but also raises concerns about accumulated errors and learning difficulty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To tackle the generation uncertainty issue of diffusers in auto-bidding for large-scale competitive auctions, we draw inspiration from the training and alignment techniques used in LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, which face a similar intrinsic dilemma of uncertainty, known as <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hallucination</span>.
Firstly, during training, we augment the denoising process by introducing an additional random variable <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, reformulating model training as learning a completer to perform masked completion.
This involves observing random <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-length historical sequences as “query” and then completing the remaining sequence as “answer”, thereby improving the dynamic legitimacy between randomly adjacent states.
However, a dynamic legitimate trajectory may still be misaligned with advertisers’ objectives due to the randomness in denoising.
Thus, during inference, we employ a trajectory-level return model as an efficient aligner to refine the final generated trajectories, aligning more closely with advertisers’ objectives.
Combining them leads to our <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ausal auto-<span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B</span>idding method based on <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>iffusion completer-aligner, termed <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CBD</span>.
Overall, our contributions could be summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1cm;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We highlight the crucial and valuable need for generative auto-bidding methods in large-scale auctions and analyze the factors hindering their development.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a novel CBD method to improve the diffuser’s decision-making performance in auto-bidding by addressing generation uncertainty in both training and inference.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments show that our method excels in large-scale real-world auto-bidding benchmarks, like achieving a notable 29.9% improvement in the challenging sparse-reward auction scenario. Given the sequential decision-making nature of our method, it could also provide insights and advantages for general offline RL tasks.
<span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">More importantly, we successfully deploy our CBD method to the Kuaishou Online advertising system and achieve a significant improvement, <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, a 2.0% increase in the target cost. </span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminary and Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Problem Statement of Auto-Bidding</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Consider a scenario where there are <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> impression opportunities arriving sequentially, each labeled by an index <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
An advertiser wins an impression if its bid <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> surpasses those advertisers and incurs a cost <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The goal is to maximize the total value of the impressions won, represented by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}o_{i}v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}o_{i}v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the impression value and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a binary variable indicating whether the advertiser wins impression <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
Additionally, it is essential to consider the budget and various KPI constraints, such as limiting the unit cost of specific advertising events like CPC and CPA.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
Therefore, the auto-bidding problem could be formulated as:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{maximize}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext mathsize="0.900em">maximize</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{maximize}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum\nolimits_{i}o_{i}v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">v</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum\nolimits_{i}o_{i}v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E1Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext mathsize="0.900em">s.t.</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum\nolimits_{i}o_{i}c_{i}\leq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum\nolimits_{i}o_{i}c_{i}\leq B</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E1Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\sum_{i}c_{ij}o_{i}}{\sum_{i}p_{ij}o_{i}}\leq C_{j},\hskip 9.24994pt\forall j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="1.097em">,</mo><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\sum_{i}c_{ij}o_{i}}{\sum_{i}p_{ij}o_{i}}\leq C_{j},\hskip 9.24994pt\forall j</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E1Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle o_{i}\in\{0,1\},\hskip 9.24994pt\forall i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="1.097em">,</mo><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle o_{i}\in\{0,1\},\hskip 9.24994pt\forall i</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the budget, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the upper bound of the <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th constraint provided by the advertiser. <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> can be any performance indicator, such as conventions, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is the cost of constraint <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
A previous study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> has shown the optimal solution:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}^{*}=\lambda_{0}v_{i}+\sum\nolimits_{j=1}^{J}\lambda_{j}p_{ij}{C_{j}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">b</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">v</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">J</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}^{*}=\lambda_{0}v_{i}+\sum\nolimits_{j=1}^{J}\lambda_{j}p_{ij}{C_{j}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the optimal bid for impression <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
The parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the optimal bidding parameters.
<span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Please note that in industrial ad platforms, auto-bidding methods typically update bidding parameters at a relatively low frequency, such as every 30 minutes, rather than for every impression opportunity.</span>
However, given the uncertain and ever-changing advertising environment, calculating these optimal bidding parameters directly is impossible.
Therefore, it stimulates research into advanced techniques for addressing this multi-constraint bidding problem in large-scale auctions, where <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a detailed literature review is in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Detailed Background ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a></span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Auto-Bidding as Decision-making</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a sequential decision-making framework where an auto-bidding agent interacts with the advertising environment <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> at discrete timesteps. At each timestep <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent receives a state <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> that describes the ad impression features and auction status, and then outputs an action <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The action is the adjustment to the bidding parameters <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}:=\{a_{t}^{\lambda_{0}},...,a_{t}^{\lambda_{J}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>J</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}:=\{a_{t}^{\lambda_{0}},...,a_{t}^{\lambda_{J}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then mapped to the final bid using Eq. (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 Problem Statement of Auto-Bidding ‣ 2 Preliminary and Related Work ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
In this context, the agent’s policy is represented as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(a_{t}|s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(a_{t}|s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The advertising environment has an underlying unknown state transition dynamic <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Without assuming an MDP, the next state could be influenced by both the historical trajectory <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and the current state.
After transitioning to the next state, the env <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> emits a reward <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which represents the value contributed to the objective within the time period <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. This process repeats until the end of a bidding period, such as one day.
A detailed description of these elements can be seen in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Details of Experiments ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
To achieve the goal in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 Problem Statement of Auto-Bidding ‣ 2 Preliminary and Related Work ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we discuss several representative related works below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Related Work.</span> As the complexity of online ad environments increased, RL became a major approach for auto-bidding.
USCB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> employs RL to maximize the value under multiple constraints.
MAAB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> considers the multi-agent RL to maximize value and social welfare.
SORL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> proposes a sustainable online RL framework to explore in the online ad system.
Recently, DiffBid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, a pioneering generative auto-bidding method, utilized a decision diffuser to create a planning and control scheme, demonstrating the remarkable potential of generative models over reinforcement learning (RL).
Despite the promising properties, some methods, such as GAS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> and GAVE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>, have found that DiffBid is ineffective in large-scale auctions.
Therefore, this paper deeply investigates this issue and explores the potential of diffusers in large-scale auctions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Diffuser for Auto-Bidding</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The diffuser is developed for decision-making based on a diffusion model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.
For simplicity, we introduce the basic elements of the decision diffuser (DD) employed by DiffBid.
DD aims to maximize likelihood estimation (MLE) of a trajectory <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> given a condition <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\mathcal{D}}[\log p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{0}(\tau)|\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi mathsize="0.900em">max</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\mathcal{D}}[\log p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{0}(\tau)|\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is the trajectory index and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> can be the property of the trajectory <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{0}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{0}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, such as the accumulated reward of the trajectory.
For brevity, we index the states from 0 to <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>,
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{{x}}_{0}=\{\boldsymbol{{s}}_{0},\boldsymbol{{s}}_{1},...,\boldsymbol{{s}}_{T}\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{{x}}_{0}=\{\boldsymbol{{s}}_{0},\boldsymbol{{s}}_{1},...,\boldsymbol{{s}}_{T}\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Specifically, DD is built with a predefined forward noising process
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\boldsymbol{x}_{k+1}(\tau)|\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau)):=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k+1}(\tau)|\sqrt{\alpha_{k}}\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau),(1-\alpha_{k})\bf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\boldsymbol{x}_{k+1}(\tau)|\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau)):=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k+1}(\tau)|\sqrt{\alpha_{k}}\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau),(1-\alpha_{k})\bf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>
and a trainable reverse denoising process
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\small p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k-1}(\tau)|\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau),\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)):=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k-1}(\tau)|\mu_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau),k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)),\sigma^{2}_{k}\bf{I}),}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">μ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\small p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k-1}(\tau)|\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau),\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)):=\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k-1}(\tau)|\mu_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau),k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)),\sigma^{2}_{k}\bf{I}),}</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a Gaussian distribution,
and
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau):=\{\boldsymbol{s}_{0}^{k},\boldsymbol{s}_{1}^{k},...,\boldsymbol{s}_{T}^{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau):=\{\boldsymbol{s}_{0}^{k},\boldsymbol{s}_{1}^{k},...,\boldsymbol{s}_{T}^{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
denotes the noisy trajectory at step <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
We could get such a DD by training on a tractable variational evidence lower bound of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{0}(\tau)|\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{0}(\tau)|\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))</annotation></semantics></math> with a surrogate loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\small{\mathcal{L}_{\text{denoise}}(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{k\sim[1,K],\boldsymbol{x}_{0}\sim\mathcal{D},\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})}\left\|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}-\epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau),k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))\right\|^{2},}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℒ</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">denoise</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∼</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">𝟎</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐈</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small{\mathcal{L}_{\text{denoise}}(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{k\sim[1,K],\boldsymbol{x}_{0}\sim\mathcal{D},\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})}\left\|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}-\epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau),k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))\right\|^{2},}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise added to the data sample <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we use a deep neural network <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k},k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k},k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))</annotation></semantics></math> to estimate the noise <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the condition <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> could be randomly dropped during training to form the classifier-free generation guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">At a timestep <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> during auto-bidding, we could do planning by generating trajectories <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> that consist of a sequence of multi-step states with expected properties and then executing actions to get bids according to the selected trajectory, leading to an explainable and reliable bidding paradigm.
Specifically, an action <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is typically obtained by inputting the observed states <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t}</annotation></semantics></math> with a horizon <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and the generated next state <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> into an inverse dynamics model <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>,
<math id="S2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}_{t}=f_{\phi}(\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t},\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}_{t}=f_{\phi}(\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t},\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1}).</annotation></semantics></math>
The inverse dynamic model could be independently trained using the training data, <span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{a}(\phi)=\mathbb{E}_{\{\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t+1},\boldsymbol{a}_{t}\}\sim\mathcal{D}}||\boldsymbol{a}_{t}-f_{\phi}(\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t},\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1})||^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℒ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">a</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϕ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒂</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒂</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒔</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msup><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{a}(\phi)=\mathbb{E}_{\{\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t+1},\boldsymbol{a}_{t}\}\sim\mathcal{D}}||\boldsymbol{a}_{t}-f_{\phi}(\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t},\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1})||^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">By combining the diffusion model with the inverse dynamic model, a decision-making policy of DiffBid is formed, <span id="S2.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>,
<math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}_{t}\sim\pi_{\theta,\phi}(\boldsymbol{a}_{t}|\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t},\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}_{t}\sim\pi_{\theta,\phi}(\boldsymbol{a}_{t}|\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t},\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Obviously, the quality of the generation of <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> directly determines the auto-bidding performance. However, we found the DD could suffer from the generation uncertainty issue due to the randomness in the denosing process. Besides, the training objective in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.3 Diffuser for Auto-Bidding ‣ 2 Preliminary and Related Work ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) lacks explicit guidance on dynamic legitimacy, resulting in the generation of next states that cannot be achieved from the previous observed states, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and experiments in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 Performance Comparison with Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, leading to inverse dynamic model outputting bad actions with these anomaly transitions. Below are some related works for addressing this issue, though not specifically for auto-bidding tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Related Work.</span> AdaptDiffuser (AD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> and MetaDiffuser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> use a forward dynamic model for guiding the generation to pursue consistent trajectories, but poor base model generation can hinder effectiveness and necessitate training revision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.
DiffuserLite (DL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> reformulates the training process as generative interpolation by training multiple diffusers to generate segment trajectories, but it could limit overall planning ability.
Adopting causal network architectures for training diffusers, such as diffusers based on RNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> or DiT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> with causal attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, is closely related to addressing this issue by enforcing the generation of next states based on historical states. However, they raise concerns about accumulated errors and learning difficulty.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Causal Auto-Bidding via Diffusion Completer-Aligner</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a novel causal auto-bidding method, termed CBD, to address the generation uncertainty issue for enhancing bidding performance in large-scale auctions by <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">i</span>) reformulating training a diffuser as learning a completer to do completion task and <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">ii</span>) aligning the generated states to advertisers’ objectives represented as the condition <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> in the inference process by refining the generated states via a trajectory-level return model as an aligner. A detailed illustration of our CBD method could be seen in Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span><span id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Completer: Learning to Complete in Training</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reflecting the large language model (LLM), when a user provides a query, the LLM completes the conversation session by generating an answer, which has demonstrated remarkable instruction-following ability in NLP tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> and inspires us to address the dynamic legitimacy issue of diffusers.
Similarly, we here use the diffuser to play such a role as a completer to fulfill an “observe and plan” session by only generating the remaining sequence of the trajectory, but in an efficient single forward generation instead of auto-regressive next-token generation of LLM, as an “answer” after receiving the observations as a “query”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The “query” in auto-bidding could be constructed by combining the historical observations with the current observation, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{0:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0:t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, at a timestep <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we start generation by sampling <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{K}(\tau)=\{\boldsymbol{s}_{0}^{K},\boldsymbol{s}_{1}^{K},...,\boldsymbol{s}_{T}^{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{K}(\tau)=\{\boldsymbol{s}_{0}^{K},\boldsymbol{s}_{1}^{K},...,\boldsymbol{s}_{T}^{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\bf{0},\bf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\bf{0},\bf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> and assign the observed states <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{s}_{0:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{s}_{0:t}</annotation></semantics></math> to the corresponding places to get the input of the diffuser, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>,</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{K}(\tau,t)=\{\underbrace{\boldsymbol{s}_{0},\ldots,\boldsymbol{s}_{t}}_{\text{Query}},\underbrace{\boldsymbol{s}_{t+1}^{K},\ldots,\boldsymbol{s}_{T}^{K}}_{\text{padding with noise}}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Query</mtext></munder><mo>,</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>padding with noise</mtext></munder><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{K}(\tau,t)=\{\underbrace{\boldsymbol{s}_{0},\ldots,\boldsymbol{s}_{t}}_{\text{Query}},\underbrace{\boldsymbol{s}_{t+1}^{K},\ldots,\boldsymbol{s}_{T}^{K}}_{\text{padding with noise}}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Note that in some offline RL tasks, previous methods always set the first position as <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and let the diffuser generate the remaining sequence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, which is not necessary for auto-bidding as the trajectory typically has a fixed length and historical information is crucial for bid assessment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
Then, according to denoising process <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k-1}(\tau)|\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau),\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{x}_{k-1}(\tau)|\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau),\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))</annotation></semantics></math>, we could sample the predicted next step’s trajectory by</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k-1}(\tau,t)=\mu_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))+\sigma_{k}\boldsymbol{z}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k-1}(\tau,t)=\mu_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))+\sigma_{k}\boldsymbol{z},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bf{z}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bf{0},\bf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bf{z}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bf{0},\bf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> could be typically set as <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{k}:=1-\alpha_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{k}:=1-\alpha_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> could be parameterized by
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{k}}}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t)-\frac{1-\alpha_{k}}{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{k}}}\boldsymbol{\hat{\epsilon}}_{k})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_{k}}}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t)-\frac{1-\alpha_{k}}{\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{k}}}\boldsymbol{\hat{\epsilon}}_{k}),</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}_{k}:=\prod_{i=1}^{k}\alpha_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}_{k}:=\prod_{i=1}^{k}\alpha_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\hat{\epsilon}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\hat{\epsilon}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> could be modeled with a conditional model <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))</annotation></semantics></math> by randomly dropping the condition <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> in a classifier-free guidance scheme <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>,</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\hat{\epsilon}}_{k}:={\epsilon}_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),k)+\omega(\epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),\boldsymbol{y}(\tau),k)-\epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),k))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\hat{\epsilon}}_{k}:={\epsilon}_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),k)+\omega(\epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),\boldsymbol{y}(\tau),k)-\epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),k)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where the scalar <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> affects the diversity of generation.
Due to the low tolerance of actions in auto-bidding, we could set <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> as 1 during inference to alleviate the generation uncertainty and enhance the property-aligned generation.
Though some works claim a larger guidance scalar <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> may decrease the legitimacy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>, we enhance it by our proposed augmented training method later.
By recursively applying the above procedure and assigning the first <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-length of the generation with the query, we could get the final generated trajectory</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{0}(\tau,t)=\{\underbrace{\boldsymbol{s}_{0},\ldots,\boldsymbol{s}_{t}}_{\text{Query}},\underbrace{\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1},\ldots,\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{T}}_{\text{Answer}}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Query</mtext></munder><mo>,</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Answer</mtext></munder><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{0}(\tau,t)=\{\underbrace{\boldsymbol{s}_{0},\ldots,\boldsymbol{s}_{t}}_{\text{Query}},\underbrace{\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1},\ldots,\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{T}}_{\text{Answer}}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Additionally, as the dynamic legitimacy issue may occur in every position of the generated trajectory, we augment the previous diffuser training procedure by setting <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as an extra random variable to construct the observations query and reformulate training as learning to complete.
Specifically, with the augmented random variable <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the completer <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> should capture the causality distribution between the observed part <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\boldsymbol{s}_{0},...,\boldsymbol{s}_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\boldsymbol{s}_{0},...,\boldsymbol{s}_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the remaining part <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\boldsymbol{s}_{t+1},...,\boldsymbol{s}_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\boldsymbol{s}_{t+1},...,\boldsymbol{s}_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math> with the causal root <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, thus the training objective is changed into</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\mathcal{D}}[\log p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{s}_{t+1:T}|\boldsymbol{s}_{0:t},\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\mathcal{D}}[\log p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{s}_{t+1:T}|\boldsymbol{s}_{0:t},\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">This objective could be seen as an augmented version of the vanilla objective of diffuser, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, replacing <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m27" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{0:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0:t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m29" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{s}_{0:T}|\emptyset,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{s}_{0:T}|\emptyset,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))</annotation></semantics></math> degenerates the above augmented objective to the vanilla one in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.3 Diffuser for Auto-Bidding ‣ 2 Preliminary and Related Work ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
To achieve this objective, we can make a small modification by introducing an augmented new completion training loss <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, while still utilizing the powerful generation ability of diffusers, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>,</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\small\mathcal{L}_{c}(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{k\sim[1,K],\boldsymbol{x}_{0}\sim\mathcal{D},{t\sim[0,T-1]},\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bf{0},\bf{I})}||\boldsymbol{\epsilon}-\epsilon_{\theta}({\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t)},k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))||_{t+1:T}^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℒ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">c</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">:=</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∼</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∼</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">𝟎</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐈</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒚</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msubsup><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\small\mathcal{L}_{c}(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{k\sim[1,K],\boldsymbol{x}_{0}\sim\mathcal{D},{t\sim[0,T-1]},\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\bf{0},\bf{I})}||\boldsymbol{\epsilon}-\epsilon_{\theta}({\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t)},k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))||_{t+1:T}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.7" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m31" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="||\cdot||_{t+1:T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">||\cdot||_{t+1:T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> means we only compute the squared loss on the position index <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in a trajectory.
With such an additional variable <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim[0,T-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim[0,T-1]</annotation></semantics></math> and replacing <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{k}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t)</annotation></semantics></math> of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.1 Completer: Learning to Complete in Training ‣ 3 Causal Auto-Bidding via Diffusion Completer-Aligner ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), the model is learned to be aware of the dynamic legitimacy of adjacent states in every positions of the trajectory and thus enhancing the dynamic legitimacy of the whole trajectory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span><span id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aligner: Refining Generations in Inference</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After training, the learned policy <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting the diffuser <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and the inverse dynamic model <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes the distribution matching error to the underlying training data distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> that was used to collect the data, <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>,
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{\theta,\phi}\text{D}_{\text{KL}}[\pi^{*}(\boldsymbol{a}_{t}|\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t},\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))||\pi_{\beta}(\boldsymbol{a}_{t}|\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t},\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mtext>D</mtext><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\theta,\phi}\text{D}_{\text{KL}}[\pi^{*}(\boldsymbol{a}_{t}|\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t},\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))||\pi_{\beta}(\boldsymbol{a}_{t}|\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t},\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))].</annotation></semantics></math>
However, there are still some concerns. First, the underlying data distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> could be biased to the property <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> in some cases, leading to the bias of the learned model; Second, the generation results can be diverse but suboptimal due to the stochastic nature of sampling-based generation, as random noise <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> are recursively employed in the generation process in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.1 Completer: Learning to Complete in Training ‣ 3 Causal Auto-Bidding via Diffusion Completer-Aligner ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> follows a complex and untractable policy distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{a}_{t}\sim f(\boldsymbol{Z}|\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t},\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)),\boldsymbol{Z}:=\{\boldsymbol{z^{k}}\sim\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{0},\boldsymbol{I})\}_{k=1:K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒁</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒁</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>𝒌</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{a}_{t}\sim f(\boldsymbol{Z}|\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t},\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)),\boldsymbol{Z}:=\{\boldsymbol{z^{k}}\sim\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{0},\boldsymbol{I})\}_{k=1:K}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is still unclear how to explicitly control the sampling process in denoising to precisely get the optimal <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, even with some advanced ODE solver methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>.
Thus, due to these concerns, the generated trajectories may raise misalignment issues in satisfying the advertiser’s expected property as shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 Performance Comparison with Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To improve the quality of the generated trajectory for enhancing the following auto-bidding performance, we introduce an aligner <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> during the inference stage.
This aligner directly refines the generation by performing a gradient update on the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1:T}</annotation></semantics></math> after the generation, utilizing a trajectory-level return model <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\varphi}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{0}(\tau,t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\varphi}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{0}(\tau,t))</annotation></semantics></math>, which could be expressed as</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1:T}^{\prime}\leftarrow\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1:T}-\lambda\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1:T}}||R_{\varphi}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{0}(\tau,t))-\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)||^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1:T}^{\prime}\leftarrow\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1:T}-\lambda\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1:T}}||R_{\varphi}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{0}(\tau,t))-\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)||^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">When the generated trajectory has a higher estimated property value <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\varphi}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{0}(\tau,t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\varphi}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{0}(\tau,t))</annotation></semantics></math> than the expected property <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> provided by the advertiser, the refinement operation would adjust the trajectory to a new one with a lower property value, and vice versa.
The return model could be independently trained to regress on the conditions <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> in the datasets expressed as</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{r}(\varphi):=\mathbb{E}_{\{\boldsymbol{x}_{0},\boldsymbol{y}\}\sim\mathcal{D}}||R_{\varphi}(\boldsymbol{x}_{0}(\tau))-\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)||^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{r}(\varphi):=\mathbb{E}_{\{\boldsymbol{x}_{0},\boldsymbol{y}\}\sim\mathcal{D}}||R_{\varphi}(\boldsymbol{x}_{0}(\tau))-\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)||^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">This regression objective could largely alleviate the issue of sparse rewards. Although the immediate reward <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for most steps may be zero, leading to a sparse reward challenge, the accumulated rewards <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sum\nolimits_{t=0:T}r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\nolimits_{t=0:T}r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are not zero and can be easy to learn.
Note that the aligner differs from the classifier-guidance denoising technique used in diffusers, which also relies on a trained return model <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>.
This technique, briefly expressed as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{k-1}\sim\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{k}+\alpha\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{k}),\sigma_{k}^{2}\boldsymbol{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{k-1}\sim\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{k}+\alpha\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{J}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{k}),\sigma_{k}^{2}\boldsymbol{I})</annotation></semantics></math>, only aims to maximize the return but can reduce generation controllablity. In auto-bidding, advertisers may prefer the strategy to be conservative sometimes to avoid winning unnecessary ad impressions at unsuitable times or with inappropriate audiences.
Moreover, the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}</annotation></semantics></math> must iteratively interact with each denoising step, creating challenges in extending it to various denoising sampling methods such as DDPM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, DDIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, and ODE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> methods.
In contrast, we refine only the final output to maintain computational efficiency and enable flexible future development, regardless of the training and inference techniques employed by the diffuser.
For instance, future directions could include multi-round refinement or exploring non-gradient methods, such as directly adding a residual to the output like LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> for optimal alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we feed the updated <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒔</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tilde{s}}_{t+1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> into the inverse dynamic model to obtain the final action.
For a better understanding of our CBD method, we provide the training and inference procedure in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2 Aligner: Refining Generations in Inference ‣ 3 Causal Auto-Bidding via Diffusion Completer-Aligner ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3.2 Aligner: Refining Generations in Inference ‣ 3 Causal Auto-Bidding via Diffusion Completer-Aligner ‣ Generative Auto-Bidding in Large-Scale Competitive Auctions via Diffusion Completer-Aligner" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.SS2.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_figure_panel ltx_float_algorithm ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_top" style="width:165.6pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> <span id="alg1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Procedure of CBD</span></figcaption>
<div id="alg1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Initialize model parameters of completer </span><math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϵ</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, aligner </span><math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, and inverse dynamic model </span><math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">for</span><span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> each iteration </span><span id="alg1.l2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">do</span><span id="alg1.l2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">   Sample </span><math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\sim[1,K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">[</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">K</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\sim[1,K]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, </span><math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\sim\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\sim\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, </span><math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim[0,T-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">[</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim[0,T-1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, </span><math id="alg1.l3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">𝟎</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐈</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l3.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">   Compute the losses:
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">       </span><math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{c}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}||\boldsymbol{\epsilon}-\epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))||_{t+1:T}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℒ</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">c</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝔼</mi><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϵ</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">τ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒚</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msubsup><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">T</mi></mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{c}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}||\boldsymbol{\epsilon}-\epsilon_{\theta}(\boldsymbol{\tilde{x}}_{k}(\tau,t),k,\boldsymbol{y}(\tau))||_{t+1:T}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">       </span><math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{r}(\varphi)=||R_{\varphi}(\boldsymbol{x}_{0}(\tau))-\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)||^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℒ</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">φ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒙</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒚</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">τ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{r}(\varphi)=||R_{\varphi}(\boldsymbol{x}_{0}(\tau))-\boldsymbol{y}(\tau)||^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">       </span><math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{a}(\phi)=||\boldsymbol{a}_{t}-f_{\phi}(\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t,\boldsymbol{s}_{t+1}})||^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℒ</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϕ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒂</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">f</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒔</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒔</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msup><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{a}(\phi)=||\boldsymbol{a}_{t}-f_{\phi}(\boldsymbol{s}_{t-L:t,\boldsymbol{s}_{t+1}})||^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">   Update </span><math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, </span><math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, </span><math id="alg1.l8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l8.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> using gradient descent on </span><math id="alg1.l8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{c}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℒ</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{c}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l8.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, </span><math id="alg1.l8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{r}(\varphi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℒ</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">φ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{r}(\varphi)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l8.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, and </span><math id="alg1.l8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{a}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">ℒ</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϕ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{a}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l8.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, respectively
</span>
</div>
